@database "stategraph"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: stategraph.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "State Graph"
@{h1}State Graph

A workflow of several agents -- one writes, one reviews, a person approves,
the draft goes back when the review fails -- written down as a state
machine in YAML instead of in a prompt. Each state does one thing: runs an
agent, calls a tool, lets a model decide, runs a Python function, waits
for an event. Transitions say where to go next, with Python guards. The
plugin runs a machine step by step, writes every step to a journal,
resumes an interrupted run without repeating finished work, and stops on
breakpoints like a debugger. You build, run and debug machines in the
@{b}State Graph@{ub} panel; the agent @{tt}stategraph_author@{utt} writes
them for you.

@{image docs/panel.png "The State Graph panel with a machine as a graph, its inspector and a run"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The State Graph panel  " link panel}
  @{"  Machines  " link machines}
  @{"  Runs  " link runs}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Tools: building machines  " link tools_machines}
  @{"  Tools: running machines  " link tools_runs}
  @{"  Tools: debugging a run  " link tools_debug}
  @{"  The authoring agent  " link author}
  @{"  Schedules  " link schedules}
  @{"  Callback URLs  " link callbacks}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Security  " link security}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The State Graph panel"
@{h1}The State Graph panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Agents & tools@{ub}, "State Graph"). It is
for admins only: anyone else is refused. A link can open a machine and a run at once:
@{tt}/plugins/stategraph/?machine=<id>&run=<run id>@{utt}.

@{image docs/panel.png "The State Graph panel"}

The panel has three parts; the toolbar buttons fold the outer two away.

@{h2}The machine list (left)

Every machine in the machine folders, in folders by the machine's
@{tt}group@{utt} (@{tt}Reviews/nightly@{utt} is a folder inside another).
A machine without a group sits in "My machines" when it lies in the
folder new machines are saved to, else in a folder named after the plugin
it comes with -- also when that folder is made writable.
Each entry shows title, id, description and a badge: @{i}valid@{ui}, the
number of errors or warnings, and @{i}read-only@{ui} for a machine outside
the writable folders. Folders you close stay closed on your next visit; a
search (id, title, description, group) opens every folder it finds
something in. @{b}New@{ub} asks for an id and creates
@{tt}<id>.yaml@{utt} with two states, @{tt}start@{utt} and
@{tt}done@{utt}.

@{h2}The machine (middle)

The head shows title, id and file, the problems (a click lists them all in
the inspector), @{b}Save@{ub} and @{b}Auto-save@{ub}, and buttons to copy
the id, @{b}Duplicate@{ub} the machine
(a copy under a new id in the folder new machines are saved to, its Python module copied
as @{tt}<id>.py@{utt}, even of a shipped machine -- the copy runs with that
folder's runner, so tools only the shipped machine's runner may call are
refused there: see @{" Runners " link setup}) and delete it (writable
machines only: its file, its layout and its Python module unless another
machine uses it; refused while another machine imports it; its runs stay
readable).

Edits are saved by hand: what you change in the graph, the inspector and
the YAML tab, and where you move states, stays unsaved until @{b}Save@{ub}
(Ctrl+S) writes it all. Opening another machine or a run of unsaved
changes asks first -- a run starts from the saved file; @{b}Duplicate@{ub}
copies the saved machine, so it waits for Save or Revert. Text typed in
the YAML tab reaches the graph with @{b}Validate@{ub}; a graph edit before
that asks first. @{b}Auto-save@{ub}
writes every edit and move at once instead; switched on, it saves what is
unsaved first. The browser remembers the choice.

@{b}Graph@{ub} -- the machine as a diagram. The bar adds a state of each
activity kind, or a plain State, Choice, Junction, Final or Composite (a
composite comes with a first state inside it); new states go into the
selected composite. A composite keeps at least one state: its last one
cannot be removed or moved out -- add or move another in first, or remove
the composite. @{b}Find a state@{ub} selects a state by name.
@{b}Undo@{ub} and @{b}Redo@{ub} put the machine back as it was before or
after an edit (the last 20 edits): the unsaved text, or with auto-save the
file. @{b}Fit@{ub}, the zoom buttons and
@{b}Auto layout@{ub} (it asks before it drops positions you dragged)
arrange the view.

@{table}
On the graph | Does
drag a state | moves it; the position is kept in a layout file next to the machine
drag a state onto a composite | puts it inside, where it was dropped (the composite is marked while it is over it)
drag a note | moves it; click it to edit its text, double-click to type into it at once
drag from a state's handle to another state | adds a transition
double-click a state | renames it; every transition and initial naming it follows
Ctrl or Shift+click | adds a state or transition to the selection
Ctrl or Shift+drag on empty canvas | selects the states wholly inside the box
Delete, Backspace | removes what is selected, after asking
Ctrl+Z; Ctrl+Shift+Z or Ctrl+Y | undo; redo
Ctrl+S | save
wheel; Shift+wheel; Ctrl+wheel | scrolls; scrolls sideways; zooms
Escape | clears the selection
@{body}

The keys work while the graph has the focus. A read-only machine can be
moved around but not changed; its layout is kept only for the open view.

@{b}YAML@{ub} -- the machine's files as text: the YAML, its Python module,
the machines it imports. Tab indents with spaces. @{b}Validate@{ub} checks
the unsaved text, @{b}Save@{ub} writes every changed file, @{b}Revert@{ub}
drops the changes -- without auto-save the graph's and the moves' too. @{b}Python module@{ub} gives a writable machine without
one its module: a @{tt}python:@{utt} line and the file, both unsaved until
Save. The problem list below the text leads to each problem's line.

@{b}Runs@{ub} -- start a run and look at the machine's runs (see below).

@{h2}The inspector (right)

@{bullet}Nothing selected: the machine's overview -- initial state, counts,
how transitions are drawn (right-angled or straight; between a composite
and a state inside it either line goes to the composite's nearest border
but the top), every problem as a
link to its place, a form for the machine's settings (title, description,
group, params, events, context, vars, imports, resources, limits, finally
...), the machines it imports, and "As an agent": which agents run this
machine, and the @{tt}agent:@{utt} block that would offer it as one (see
@{" Machines " link machines}).
@{bullet}A state: @{b}Rename@{ub}, @{b}Initial@{ub} (makes it the first
state of its region), @{b}Remove@{ub}; its description, a box of free
text for whoever reads the machine (lines of its own are kept); breakpoints on enter, exit or error
(only the hooks that can stop there -- a choice or a top-level final has
none, and the panel says why); its activity (the kind, and a field for
each setting of that kind, with the agents, tools and decision profiles
there are to pick from); its settings (type, max_visits, timeout, entry,
exit, finally, a final's status and output); its transitions, in the
order they are tried; and its YAML, to edit the state as text.
@{b}Inside@{ub} names the composite it sits in: another one, or the top
level, moves it there -- out of a composite too, which dragging cannot.
@{bullet}A note: its text, @{b}Apply@{ub} and @{b}Remove@{ub}. @{b}Note@{ub}
in the bar above the graph adds one in the middle of the view. Notes are
free text in the file, under @{tt}notes:@{utt}; where each one sits is
kept in the layout file, and the engine never reads them. A note emptied
and applied is removed, after asking.
@{bullet}A transition: trigger (with @{b}New event...@{ub}, which declares
a new event and picks it), target, guard, effect, and how its line is
drawn; move it up or down in the order, or remove it.
@{bullet}Several states and transitions: how their lines are drawn,
@{b}Group@{ub} (puts the states into a new composite) and
@{b}Remove@{ub}.

Every form has its own @{b}Apply@{ub}; it sends only the fields you
changed, and the file's comments stay. What you typed into the other
forms stays through an edit. The panel asks before it drops text you did
not apply: when you select something else, reload the machine, or apply
another edit while the state's YAML box holds text.

@{h2}Starting a run

In the Runs tab: a field per param of the machine (the last values you
started it with come back), @{b}Mocks@{ub} (JSON: a state path and the
answer its activity gives instead of running, see @{" Runs " link runs}),
@{b}Mock only@{ub} (anything not mocked fails instead of calling an agent
or a tool) and @{b}Pause at start@{ub}. Breakpoints and watches you set
before the run go with it. @{b}Start@{ub} shows the new run.

@{h2}Watching a run

Pick a run in the list (filter by status; @{b}Older runs@{ub} loads
more). While it is alive the panel reads it every second; everything else
reloads every 10 seconds (the menu next to the reload button changes it),
the run list every 3 seconds while another of its runs is alive.

@{bullet}The @{b}debug bar@{ub} above the tabs: status, where it is paused
or ended. Other runs alive in the run list (as filtered and loaded) stand
beside the one shown, each with its status; a click shows another.
@{b}Continue@{ub}, @{b}Step@{ub} (to the next hook),
@{b}Pause@{ub}, @{b}Run to@{ub} a state, @{b}Terminate@{ub} (its finally
activities still run), @{b}Resume@{ub} for an interrupted run, and
@{b}Fork@{ub}: a new run that replays this one up to a top-level step and
goes on live from there (with Shift: on the machine as it is now, not as
it was when the run started). A run waiting for an event has a button per
event it takes. "in another process" marks a run another process runs:
you see its journal; Pause, Continue, Step and Terminate reach it within a
second, Run to and breakpoints only in its own process.
@{bullet}The @{b}graph@{ub} marks the states the run is in, and the states
with breakpoints.
@{bullet}@{b}Submachines@{ub}: with a run selected, a state that started
submachine runs in it carries a badge ("2 runs"). Click it: the
inspector's last section, folded, lists its @{b}Submachine runs@{ub}
("critique_round #2", "ended in done"); @{b}Show@{ub} opens the submachine's
graph with that run on it -- where it stands, what it visited, its last
transition. The frames of the Debug tab and the end states of the Result
card have the same button (not for a machine of a file's
@{tt}machines:@{utt}, which has no graph of its own). On the submachine's
graph the debug bar picks among its frames in the run ("under review
#2", "running") and its arrow goes back to the run's machine; breakpoints and
Run to set there are the submachine's. Its runs list also holds the runs
it ran in ("in scene_review"): one picked shows the frame picked, else a
live one, else the last. A fork shows the frames it ran itself; those
before its fork point are in the run it was forked from.
@{bullet}The @{b}Result@{ub} card: output or error, the final state of each
frame, and every finished activity folded -- open one for its full answer,
its input, failed attempts, the agent's session (@{b}Open@{ub} shows the
conversation in the chat) and the request id (the message debugger finds
the LLM calls by it). @{b}Run again@{ub} starts a new run with the same
params and mocks; @{b}Session@{ub} opens the run's own session (not for
a mock-only run, which has none, nor for another user's run).
@{bullet}The @{b}History@{ub} card: the last 200 journal rows, filtered by
kind.
@{bullet}The @{b}Debug@{ub} tab of the inspector: the run's params,
output and error; watches (a watch pauses the run when its value
changes); breakpoints; @{b}Evaluate@{ub} and @{b}Set@{ub} while paused
(Set writes into @{tt}ctx@{utt} -- or, at an exit or error breakpoint,
sets the activity's answer); @{b}Send event@{ub} with data; and every
frame with its state, step and @{tt}ctx@{utt} (@{b}Show@{ub} shows it on
its machine's graph).

Watches and breakpoints set while no run is live belong to the next run
of this machine and are kept in the browser; set during a run they change
that run only.

@{h2}When the server says no

@{bullet}An edit to a file that changed since the panel read it (another
admin, an agent): "The file changed" -- @{b}Reload@{ub} shows it as it is
now, @{b}Cancel@{ub} keeps your text so you can copy it first.
@{bullet}A save with errors: @{b}Save anyway@{ub} writes it; a machine with
errors does not run.
@{bullet}A new file whose name is taken by a file the machine does not
use: @{b}Use the existing file@{ub} or cancel.
@{bullet}A run that cannot be read for a moment: the bar says "not
refreshed" and the panel keeps trying.
@endnode

@node machines "Machines"
@{h1}Machines

A machine is one YAML file, @{tt}<id>.yaml@{utt}, in a machine folder.
Here is a whole one: an agent builds a change, a second agent judges it,
a person approves or sends it back with notes.

@{code yaml}
stategraph: 1
id: build_review
title: Build and review
params:
  task: {type: string, required: true, description: What to build}
events:
  approve: {description: A person accepts the change}
  reject: {description: A person sends it back with notes, data: {type: string}}
context: {change: null, notes: null}
initial: build
states:
  build:
    max_visits: 3
    do:
      agent: coder
      task: "{{ params.task }} -- notes from the last review: {{ ctx.notes }}"
      timeout: 10m
    transitions:
      - target: review
        effect: ctx.change = out
      - trigger: error
        target: failed
  review:
    do:
      decide: noul
      by: reviewer
      question: Is this change ready to merge?
      input: "{{ ctx.change }}"
    transitions:
      - target: approval
        guard: out["value"] >= 0.7
      - target: build
        guard: else
  approval:
    timeout: 24h
    transitions:
      - trigger: approve
        target: done
      - trigger: reject
        target: build
        effect: ctx.notes = event.data
      - trigger: error
        target: failed
  done: {type: final, output: {change: "{{ ctx.change }}"}}
  failed: {type: final, status: failed}
@{body}

@{h2}The parts

@{bullet}@{b}params@{ub} are the run's input, @{b}context@{ub}
(@{tt}ctx@{utt}) its variables, @{b}events@{ub} what may come from
outside.
@{bullet}A @{b}state@{ub} with @{tt}do:@{utt} runs one activity; when it
ends, its answer is @{tt}out@{utt} and the transitions without a trigger
are tried in order. A failure is the event @{tt}error@{utt}.
@{bullet}A state without an activity whose transitions all wait for
events is a @{b}wait state@{ub}; its @{tt}timeout@{utt} raises the error
@{tt}wait_timeout@{utt}. A state with @{tt}after: 10m@{utt} is a
@{b}timer@{ub}: it goes on by itself after that time.
@{bullet}@{b}Guards@{ub} and @{b}effects@{ub} are plain Python over
@{tt}params@{utt}, @{tt}ctx@{utt}, @{tt}out@{utt}, @{tt}event@{utt} and
@{tt}error@{utt}; @{tt}else@{utt} is the last guard. Texts are templates
(@{tt}{{ ctx.change }}@{utt}).
@{bullet}A @{b}final@{ub} state ends the run -- with status
@{tt}failed@{utt} as a failure -- and gives its @{tt}output@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Choice@{ub} and @{b}junction@{ub} states branch by guards,
@{b}composite@{ub} states hold states of their own, @{tt}max_visits@{utt}
bounds a loop (one entry more raises @{tt}loop_limit@{utt}),
@{tt}finally@{utt} runs an activity on every way out.
@{bullet}@{tt}python: <id>.py@{utt} names a @{b}companion module@{ub}: its
public functions are in scope in guards, effects and templates, and can be
@{tt}call@{utt} activities.

@{h2}Handling errors in one place

A transition on a composite holds for every state inside it. An
@{tt}error@{utt}, and every named event, is offered to the active state
first, then to its composite, then to that one's composite and so on
outward, each in list order. So one @{tt}error@{utt} transition on a
composite catches what its states do not handle themselves; a state that
does handle an error (a retry on @{tt}wait_timeout@{utt}, say) keeps it.
A guard picks the kinds: @{tt}error.type == "tool_failed"@{utt}. In the
panel, drag from the composite's handle, top right of its box.

For the whole machine, put its states into one composite: select them all
(Ctrl+drag a box around them) and click @{b}Group@{ub}; they keep their
places. Beside it, a final state with @{tt}status: failed@{utt} takes the
errors (the @{tt}do:@{utt} of plan and write left out here):

@{code yaml}
initial: main
states:
  main:
    initial: plan
    states:
      plan: {transitions: [{target: write}]}
      write: {transitions: [{target: done}]}
    transitions:
      - trigger: error
        target: aborted
  done: {type: final}
  aborted: {type: final, status: failed}
@{body}

The machine itself has no transitions. An error nothing handles ends the
run as failed anyway; @{tt}finally:@{utt} on the machine runs on every
end, for cleanup. Completion is the exception to the rule: a state that
finishes takes only its own transitions without a trigger, never its
composite's -- the composite completes when one of its final states is
entered.

@{h2}Activity kinds

@{table}
Kind | Does | out
agent | runs a configured agent with a task, in a session of its own | its answer (parsed with schema or parse)
tool | calls a tool directly, no LLM; only tools the runner agent may call | the tool's result
decide | asks the decision model, a decision profile or an agent (by) a question | value, confidence, probabilities
call | calls a Python function of the companion module | its return value
machine | runs another machine (imports) in a frame of its own | that machine's final output
parallel | runs several activities at once | an object with one answer per branch
map | runs one activity per item of a list | the answers in item order
emit | tells the caller and the run's session how far the run is | the text
callback | makes a URL that sends one event to this run, once (see @{" Callback URLs " link callbacks}) | url, event, expires
@{body}

Every activity also takes @{tt}retry@{utt}, @{tt}timeout@{utt} (per
attempt) and @{tt}idempotent@{utt}. The complete format -- every field,
the scope of each code field, the validation codes -- is in
@{" the format cheat sheet " link docs/format.md} and the authoring
skill's reference, @{tt}skills/stategraph-authoring/references/format.md@{utt};
new activity kinds: @{" Extending " link docs/extending.md}. The plugin
ships examples in its @{tt}machines/@{utt} folder: @{tt}hello_agent@{utt},
@{tt}approval@{utt}, @{tt}critique_round@{utt},
@{tt}fan_out_review@{utt}, @{tt}scene_review@{utt} and
@{tt}showcase@{utt}, which uses every element of the format once.

@{h2}Validation

A machine is checked whenever it is listed, opened, saved or started:
format, graph (unreachable states, dead ends), Python (it compiles, the
names exist, code fields stay pure), each activity's fields, and whether
this installation can run it -- agents that exist, tools the runner may
call. Each problem carries a level, a code (@{tt}SG002@{utt} ...), a
message that names the fix, the path in the file and the line. A machine
with errors is not started.

@{h2}A machine as an agent

An @{tt}agent:@{utt} block in the machine's file offers it as an agent: a
SAM can spawn it, the chat can list it, other agents call it by name, and
the answer is the run's output as JSON. This is the plugin
@{tt}stategraph_machine@{utt}; the block takes effect when a process
starts. The panel's overview shows the block to copy and whether it is
declared.
@endnode

@node runs "Runs"
@{h1}Runs

@{h2}Statuses

@{table}
Status | Means | What goes on
running | it works | by itself
paused | a breakpoint, a watch, a step or Pause holds it | continue, step, run_to
waiting | a wait state waits for an event, or a timer state for its time | send an event; a timer goes on by itself
interrupted | the process that ran it stopped | resume
succeeded | it reached a final state | --
failed | a failed final state, or an error no transition took | --
cancelled | terminated | --
@{body}

@{h2}The journal

Every step goes into @{tt}runs.db@{utt}: each activity's input and answer,
each transition with the guards it evaluated, events, timers, edits made
in the debugger. That is what the panel's history and result show -- and
what makes a run survive its process. A process that stops marks its
runs @{tt}interrupted@{utt}; one that crashes leaves them
@{tt}running@{utt} until its lease on them has run out (a minute), then
the next process that looks marks them. A schedule resumes its slot's
run, and a callback URL resumes its run before it sends the event.
Nothing else resumes by itself: resume the run (panel,
@{tt}control_run@{utt}), or start it again with its run key. A resumed run replays its journal: an
activity that finished is not run again, its answer is read. One that was
in flight is started again only if it is idempotent (the default; a
@{tt}tool@{utt} activity is not, unless it says so) -- otherwise it fails
with @{tt}interrupted@{utt}.

@{h2}Mocks

A test run can answer activities from a mock instead of running them. A
mock is keyed by the state path (@{tt}review@{utt}, or
@{tt}summarise/0@{utt} for the first item of a map):

@{code json}
{"build": {"$visits": ["patch v1", "patch v2"]},
 "review": {"$visits": [{"value": 0.2}, {"value": 0.9}]},
 "publish": {"$error": {"type": "tool_failed", "message": "down"}}}
@{body}

@{tt}$visits@{utt} answers each use of the path in turn, and the last one
for every use after that; @{tt}$error@{utt} fails the activity. On a wait
state with a @{tt}timeout@{utt}, or a timer state (@{tt}after@{utt}),
@{tt}{"$timeout": true}@{utt} makes its time up at once: a test drives the
timeout path without waiting it out.
With @{b}mock only@{ub}, an agent, tool or decide activity without a mock
fails with @{tt}unmocked@{utt}; a @{tt}call@{utt} activity runs, as it is
local Python. A run lists the mocks it never used in
@{tt}mocks_unused@{utt}.

@{h2}Terminate, fork, run again

@{bullet}@{b}Terminate@{ub} cancels what runs and then runs the
@{tt}finally@{utt} activities; the run ends @{tt}cancelled@{utt}. An
interrupted run can be terminated too: it is resumed into its end.
@{bullet}@{b}Fork@{ub} makes a new run that replays the source's journal up
to a top-level step and goes on live -- with the source's definition, or
the machine as it is now. Stores, databases and agent conversations keep
what the source did; the fork is a run of whoever forked it.
@{bullet}A @{b}run key@{ub} (@{tt}run_key@{utt}) makes a start the same
request again: see @{" Tools: running machines " link tools_runs}.

@{h2}The run's session

A run that is not mock-only has a chat session of its own,
@{tt}sg_<run id>@{utt}: a message with its params when it starts, one with
status, final state and output or error when it ends, and each agent it
ran as a sub-session below it. It is read-only in the chat. A run started
by an agent's tool call sits below that agent's session.

@{h2}Who sees a run

With auth on, a run belongs to the user who started it: others get it
answered as missing, admins see every run, and a run of nobody (a schedule
without a user) is visible to all. A resumed or terminated run runs as its
own user, whoever triggered it.
@endnode

@node tools_machines "Tools: building machines"
@{h1}Tools: building machines

The tools are named after the plugin instance: @{tt}stategraph_catalog@{utt}
and so on in the default setup. Every answer carries @{tt}status@{utt}
@{tt}success@{utt}, or @{tt}error@{utt} with @{tt}error@{utt} (the reason)
and @{tt}error_type@{utt} (@{tt}http_404@{utt}, @{tt}http_409@{utt},
@{tt}http_422@{utt}). Validate and save need an admin or a user in
@{tt}allowed_users@{utt} when auth is on; the others are open.

@{h2}stategraph_catalog

What a machine may use.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
agents | fnmatch pattern on the agent names; default every agent
tools | fnmatch pattern on the flat tool name; default every tool
machine_id | the machine whose runner names the tools (its folder decides); default a new machine's
@{body}

Answer: @{tt}kinds@{utt} (key, summary, and each field with its
description), @{tt}agents@{utt} (name, description: every enabled agent a
machine may run -- not a runner, not a machine agent, not an agent that
can use a stategraph tool beyond the read-only ones), @{tt}runner@{utt}, @{tt}tools@{utt} (every
tool the runner's allowlist lets through: flat name, description,
parameters, required), @{tt}decision_profiles@{utt} and
@{tt}examples@{utt} (the ids of the machines the plugins ship: all but
those in the folder new machines are saved to).

@{h2}stategraph_list_machines

No parameters. @{tt}machines@{utt}: id, title, description, group, file,
root (its machine folder), writable, valid, and the numbers of errors and
warnings.

@{h2}stategraph_get_machine

@{tt}machine_id@{utt} (required). Answer: @{tt}files@{utt} (relative path
to text: the YAML, its Python module, the machines it imports), @{tt}versions@{utt} (relative path to version -- pass them back
when you save), @{tt}problems@{utt}, @{tt}writable@{utt},
@{tt}agents@{utt} (the machine agents that run it), @{tt}offer@{utt}
(its @{tt}agent:@{utt} block's name, and whether it is declared) and
@{tt}runner@{utt} (the runner its runs get; @{tt}runner_problem@{utt} when
two claim its folder).

@{h2}stategraph_validate_machine

Checks without saving.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
files | relative path to text; the root is <machine_id>.yaml
yaml | one machine file instead of files
machine_id | the root machine; default the id in yaml, or the one file <id>.yaml whose id says so
@{body}

Answer: @{tt}machine_id@{utt}, @{tt}problems@{utt} (level, code, message,
path, file, line), @{tt}valid@{utt} (no error).

@{h2}stategraph_save_machine

Validates, then writes all files or none next to the machine's file; a
new machine goes into the folder new machines are saved to.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
files | required; relative path to text
machine_id | as for validate
expected_versions | relative path to the version get_machine gave, for every existing file you change
@{body}

Refused with the first three errors when validation finds errors
(@{tt}http_422@{utt}); refused when a file changed since you read it, or
when you change a file of the machine without its version
(@{tt}http_409@{utt}, "pass its version from get_machine in
expected_versions"), or when a file of the save would hide another
machine: a @{tt}<x>.yaml@{utt} written into a machine folder searched
before the one machine x lies in (@{tt}http_409@{utt}, "would hide
machine"); refused for a machine outside the writable folder
(@{tt}http_403@{utt}: save a copy under a new id). Answer:
@{tt}machine_id@{utt}, the new @{tt}versions@{utt}, @{tt}problems@{utt}
(warnings). No tool deletes a machine; the panel does.

@{code json}
{"machine_id": "build_review"}
{"files": {"build_review.yaml": "stategraph: 1\nid: build_review\n..."}, "expected_versions": {"build_review.yaml": "0a6ba1974579e791"}}
@{body}
@endnode

@node tools_runs "Tools: running machines"
@{h1}Tools: running machines

@{tt}stategraph_run_machine@{utt} needs an admin or a user in
@{tt}allowed_users@{utt} when auth is on; @{tt}stategraph_get_run@{utt}
and @{tt}stategraph_list_runs@{utt} are open, but show only the runs the
caller may see (see @{" Runs " link runs}).

@{h2}stategraph_run_machine

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
machine_id | the machine to run
request | instead of machine_id: "<id> {json params}" or "<id> key=value ..."; a value reads as the param's declared type (n=3, flag=true), quotes group words
params | the run's input; with request, they win over the request's
mocks | state path to answer; see Runs
mock_only | true: no agent, tool or decision without a mock
breakpoints | "state", "state\@exit", or {state, at, condition, machine}
watchpoints | Python expressions, or {expr, condition, machine}
pause_at_start | true: hold the run at its first hook
run_key | the same request again (below)
wait | finish (default): return when the run ends, pauses or waits for an event; background: return at once
max_wait | 0 to 3600 seconds; default the server's default_max_wait (600)
full_output | true: the output in full; else texts over 20,000 characters are cut
@{body}

A machine with errors is refused (@{tt}http_422@{utt}, the first three
errors). The answer: @{tt}run_id@{utt}, @{tt}run_status@{utt},
@{tt}state@{utt}, @{tt}output@{utt}, @{tt}error@{utt} (type, message,
state, cause), @{tt}paused@{utt}, @{tt}accepts@{utt} (the events each
frame takes now), @{tt}mocks_unused@{utt}, and @{tt}next@{utt}: what the
run asks of you -- wait with get_run, send an event, continue, or resume.
@{tt}status@{utt} success only says the call worked; whether the run did
is @{tt}run_status@{utt}. A caller that is cancelled while it waits takes
its run with it (terminated).

@{b}run_key.@{ub} Every call looks up the newest run of the key: a run this
process runs is attached to (@{tt}attached: true@{utt}); one another
process runs is reported (@{tt}attached: false@{utt}, nothing started);
an interrupted one is resumed (@{tt}resumed: true@{utt}); an ended one
answers again with @{tt}ended@{utt}: its status, output or error. Only a
run that failed transiently (@{tt}interrupted@{utt}, @{tt}timeout@{utt},
@{tt}agent_failed@{utt}, @{tt}decision_failed@{utt}, @{tt}internal@{utt},
@{tt}diverged@{utt}) starts a new run. The same key with another machine,
params, mocks or mock_only is refused, as is a key of another user's run.

@{h2}stategraph_get_run

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
run_id | required
steps | how many journal rows: 1 to 500, default 30
after | the rows after this journal seq (to page forward); without it, the last ones
kinds | only rows of these kinds: activity, trace, event, edit, timer
state | only rows of this state
wait | now (default), or finish: wait while the run is running or in a timer
max_wait | as for run_machine
full_output | as for run_machine
@{body}

The answer adds to run_machine's: @{tt}frames@{utt} (each frame's
machine, state, step, visits, @{tt}ctx@{utt} and params, what it waits
for), @{tt}inbox@{utt} (events no frame has taken yet),
@{tt}journal@{utt} and @{tt}debug@{utt} (breakpoints, watchpoints, their
values, where it is paused). Texts in frames, journal and error are cut at
2,000 characters. An answer over 200,000 characters drops the oldest
journal rows first (@{tt}journal_cut@{utt} says how many), then shows each
frame's ctx as its keys and their sizes (@{tt}frames_cut@{utt}). A reader
that is cancelled while it waits leaves the run alone. A reader who is
neither the run's user nor an admin gets every callback URL's token as
@{tt}<hidden>@{utt} (see @{" Callback URLs " link callbacks}).

@{h2}stategraph_list_runs

The newest runs the caller may see, newest first.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
machine_id | only runs of this machine
status | only runs in this status
limit | 1 to 200, default 20
@{body}

@{tt}runs@{utt}: id, machine_id, status, final_state, created_at,
finished_at, run_key, parent_run (a fork's source) and a failed run's
error message (300 characters).

@{h2}Slash commands

@{table}
Command | Does
/stategraph-run <id> [{json} or key=value ...] | starts a run in the background
/stategraph-runs [machine id] | the newest runs you may see
/stategraph-stop <run id> | terminates a run; its finally activities run
@{body}

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"machine_id": "build_review", "params": {"task": "Add a retry to the fetcher"}, "mock_only": true,
 "mocks": {"build": "patch v1", "review": {"value": 0.9}}}
{"run_id": "7f449c8473f4", "kinds": ["activity"], "steps": 20}
{"request": "build_review task='Add a retry to the fetcher'", "wait": "background"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node tools_debug "Tools: debugging a run"
@{h1}Tools: debugging a run

Both tools need an admin or a user in @{tt}allowed_users@{utt} when auth
is on, and act only on runs the caller may see.

@{h2}stategraph_control_run

@{table}
action | Needs | Does
pause | -- | stops at the next hook
continue | -- | goes on from a pause
step | -- | goes on to the next hook
run_to | state, machine | goes on until that state is entered
terminate | -- | ends the run; its finally activities run first (the answer waits up to 10 s)
resume | -- | an interrupted run goes on from its journal
fork | at_step, definition, pause, mocks | a new run from a top-level step
set_breakpoints | breakpoints | replaces the run's breakpoints
set_watchpoints | watchpoints | replaces the run's watchpoints
evaluate | expr | a read-only Python expression in the paused scope
set | path, expr | while paused: ctx.<path> = expr; or out at an exit or error breakpoint
@{body}

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
run_id, action | required
state, machine | run_to: the state, and the machine it belongs to (default any frame)
at_step | fork: 0 or more; default where the source's journal ends
definition | fork: snapshot (default, the run's own) or current (the machine now; refused if it has errors)
pause | fork: hold the fork at the fork point
mocks | fork: mocks over the source's; what the fork replays keeps its answer
breakpoints, watchpoints | as for run_machine
expr, path | evaluate and set
steps | 1 to 500: the answer carries that many journal rows; without it none
@{body}

Every argument is checked before anything acts; a wrong type, or an
argument the table does not name, is refused (@{tt}http_422@{utt}). Evaluate and set on a run that
is not paused, or not in this process, are refused (@{tt}http_409@{utt}). Pause, continue, step
and terminate reach a run another process holds: it takes them within a
second, and one it does not take within 5 seconds is withdrawn and
refused (@{tt}http_409@{utt}), as is a second request while one is open;
run_to and breakpoints act in the owning process only.
The answer is the run as run_machine gives it plus @{tt}action@{utt} --
for evaluate and set @{tt}value@{utt}, for fork the new
@{tt}run_id@{utt} and @{tt}forked_from@{utt}. The debugger's state (a
pause, a step, run_to) survives an interruption and a resume.

@{h2}stategraph_send_event

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
run_id, name | required; name is an event some machine of the run declares
data | the event's payload; checked against the event's declared data schema
frame | the frame to send it to, when several accept it
@{body}

@{tt}accepted: true@{utt} with the @{tt}frame@{utt} that takes it. A frame
that waits has taken it when the answer comes, so a @{tt}get_run@{utt}
right after shows what the event started; a frame busy with an activity
takes it when it next waits. An event no frame accepts yet is kept in the
run's inbox (@{tt}queued: true@{utt}) until one does. Refused (@{tt}http_409@{utt}): an undeclared event, data
that does not fit ("data does not match its declared schema"), several
frames that accept it without @{tt}frame@{utt}, a run that has ended or
runs in another process.
@endnode

@node author "The authoring agent"
@{h1}The authoring agent

@{tt}stategraph_author@{utt} writes machines. Give it a workflow in words;
it looks up what it may use (catalog), writes the files, validates them
(at most five rounds), saves them, and proves the machine with a
mock-only test run that must reach the intended final state (at most three
rounds). It hands over the machine id, the files, the validation result,
the test run, what the mocks proved and what they cannot prove (prompts,
real tool behaviour, timings), and what an operator has to configure.
Anything short of a saved, clean, proven machine is handed over as NOT
PROVEN.

It starts a live run only when you ask for exactly that. It sees the
chat (@{tt}visibility: both@{utt}); another agent reaches it through its
SAM when that SAM lists @{tt}stategraph_author@{utt} in
@{tt}allowed_agents@{utt}. Its saves go through the same checks as
anyone's: with auth on, the user it works for must be an admin or in
@{tt}allowed_users@{utt}.
@endnode

@node schedules "Schedules"
@{h1}Schedules

Machines the clock starts, in the server entry:

@{code yaml}
stategraph:
  schedules:
    - name: nightly_review
      machine: build_review
      every: 24h        # slots from 00:00 UTC in steps of every ...
      offset: 3h        # ... plus offset: 03:00 UTC
      late: 1h          # a slot first seen later than this after its start is left out
      params: {task: "Tidy the nightly build"}
      user: admin       # whose run it is; default nobody's (every user sees it)
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
name | required | a name: lowercase letters, digits, _
machine | required | the machine id
every | required | at least 1m
offset | 0 | shifts the slots
late | every, at most 1h | at least 1m
params | {} | the run's params
user | none | the run's user
@{body}

A slot runs once: its run key is @{tt}schedule:<name>:<slot>@{utt}. After
a transient failure it runs again 5 minutes after the last attempt ended,
three attempts in all; a slot whose run was interrupted is resumed. One
process starts the slots of an instance -- the one that holds the
scheduler lease in @{tt}runs.db@{utt}; another takes over when that
process stops. A wrong entry is logged as an error and left out.
@endnode

@node callbacks "Callback URLs"
@{h1}Callback URLs

A @{tt}callback@{utt} activity makes a URL that sends one event to its
run, once -- for a system outside to answer a wait: a link in a mail, a
webhook.

@{code yaml}
ask:
  do: {callback: approve, expires: 48h}
  transitions:
    - target: waiting
      effect: ctx.link = out["url"]
@{body}

@{tt}out@{utt} is @{tt}{url, event, expires}@{utt}; the event must be one
the machine declares; @{tt}expires@{utt} defaults to 168h, at most 720h.
The URL is @{tt}<public_url>/plugins/stategraph/callback?token=...@{utt}
-- a path without @{tt}public_url@{utt}.

@{bullet}GET shows a page that says what the link sends, with a button:
a mail scanner that opens the link sends nothing.
@{bullet}POST sends the event, with an optional JSON body
@{tt}{"data": ...}@{utt} of at most 64 KB. The link is used up when the
run took the event (or keeps it in its inbox); an interrupted run is
resumed first. A link that is unknown, used, expired, or whose run ended
answers 404; one the run cannot take right now answers 409 and stays
usable; data that does not fit the event's schema answers 422.

The token is the key. The table that holds the callbacks keeps only its
hash, but the URL itself is the activity's answer: it is in the run's
journal in plain text, and in whatever the machine keeps it in
(@{tt}ctx@{utt}, the output). @{tt}get_run@{utt} shows it only to an
admin and to the run's own user; anyone else who may see the run -- a run
of nobody, such as a schedule's without @{tt}user@{utt} -- gets
@{tt}<hidden>@{utt} in place of the token. The route
is admin-only like the rest of the plugin until an operator opens it --
see @{" Security " link security}.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Tool and parameter descriptions come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; the catalog's field descriptions from the activity
kinds. The chat's status line ends with the result: "run 7f449c8473f4:
waiting in approval", "build_review: 1 error(s), 2 warning(s)", "saved
build_review: 1 file(s), 0 error(s), 0 warning(s)", "event accepted by
frame root", "catalog: 9 kinds, 4 agents, 12 tools". Refusals say what to
change:

@{bullet}"stategraph: only admins may use run_machine (user alice);
machines contain Python and run agents and tools. An operator can list the
user in the instance's allowed_users."
@{bullet}"1 error(s), not saved: build_review.yaml:11 SG002 transition target
'nowhere' is not a state of this machine"
@{bullet}"build_review.yaml is a file of machine 'build_review' already:
pass its version from get_machine in expected_versions to change it"
@{bullet}"run_key 'k1' names run 0b802a51bef9 with other params; the same
key is the same request -- use a new key for a new one"
@{bullet}"max_wait must be a number from 0 to 3600, not 4000"
@{bullet}A failed run's error names the state and the cause:
"review: mock-only run and no mock for this decide activity"
(@{tt}unmocked@{utt}).

@{h2}The authoring agent

@{tt}stategraph_author@{utt}'s system prompt is its own prompt (about 4 KB)
and the body of the skill @{tt}stategraph-authoring@{utt} (about 11 KB):
the method, one whole machine, the rules, the kinds, the mock syntax, the
validation codes. It reads the full format reference (about 59 KB),
@{tt}patterns.md@{utt} (20 KB) and @{tt}debugging.md@{utt} (15 KB) on
demand; those are appended to the conversation. @{tt}context_engineer@{utt}
is on for it.

@{h2}Agents a machine runs

They see their task and the template vars the machine gives them, nothing
about the plugin. An agent that decides (@{tt}decide@{utt} with
@{tt}by@{utt}) gets the question, the form of the answer and the input, and
answers in JSON. A runner agent (@{tt}stategraph_runner@{utt} or a
plugin's own) is never talked to; its prompt only tells a stray visitor
where to go.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool answers are ordinary tool results, appended; nothing earlier
changes. They grow with the machine: @{tt}get_machine@{utt} returns every
file, @{tt}get_run@{utt} up to @{tt}steps@{utt} journal rows (at most
200,000 characters). The author's system prompt holds no ticking values:
a stable cache prefix. Runs cost the author no tokens -- the engine calls
agents and tools itself. The plugin has no hook: it writes only into its
runs' own sessions.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}An agent activity's cost counts its LLM calls and its
sub-agents'; LLM calls a tool makes on its own are not in it. A call with
neither a billed cost nor a price for its model counts in
@{tt}cost_unpriced_calls@{utt}, not in @{tt}cost@{utt}.
@{bullet}Of a run another process holds, pause, continue, step and
terminate reach it; run_to, breakpoints, evaluate and set act only in its
own process.
@{bullet}A synchronous @{tt}call@{utt} function runs in a worker thread:
terminate and timeouts end the activity at once, and Python cannot stop
the thread -- a long function checks @{tt}sg.cancelled@{utt} (a
@{tt}threading.Event@{utt}, set once nobody waits for its answer) and
returns early.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin ships its own configuration,
@{tt}agents/stategraph.yaml@{utt} and
@{tt}agents/stategraph_author.yaml@{utt}, which
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} includes
(@{tt}../src/plugins*/*/agents/*.yaml@{utt}); nothing needs registering.
The entry of the tool instance, without its comments and the lines for
other plugins' machines (an @{tt}inject_params@{utt} entry among them):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    stategraph:
      type: stategraph
      enabled: true
      description: "State machines for agent workflows: model, validate, run and debug them"
      machine_dirs:
        - data/stategraph/machines        # made in the panel; not versioned
        - "src/plugins*/*/machines"        # shipped with their plugin
      writable_machine_dirs:
        - data/stategraph/machines
      runs_db: data/stategraph/runs.db
      runner_agent: stategraph_runner
      allowed_users: []
      default_max_wait: 600
@{body}

It comes with @{tt}stategraph_runner@{utt} (the default runner: hosts the
runs; its tool allowlist is what a @{tt}tool@{utt} activity may call),
@{tt}stategraph_json@{utt} (a JSON store for machines' documents, kept
without expiry so a resumed run finds them), @{tt}stategraph_author@{utt}
and @{tt}stategraph_example_agent@{utt} (the plain agent of the example
machines).

@{h2}Letting machines use more

@{bullet}An @{tt}agent@{utt} activity runs any configured, enabled agent the
machine names -- no entry anywhere. A new agent needs a restart.
@{bullet}A tool goes into the allowlist of the machine's @{b}runner@{ub} --
never a stategraph tool: validation refuses them (SG007), so a machine
cannot save, start or control machines.
@{bullet}A secret a tool needs goes into @{tt}inject_params@{utt}, never
into the machine: @{tt}{tool pattern: {param: value}}@{utt}, fnmatch on
the flat tool name, applied after the templates; the config's value wins,
and a tool result that echoes it is redacted.

@{h2}Runners

Every run is hosted by a runner agent, and its tool allowlist is all a
@{tt}tool@{utt} activity (or @{tt}sg.tool()@{utt}) may call. Which runner a
machine gets depends on its @{b}folder@{ub}: the runner whose
@{tt}runs_machines_in@{utt} names the folder, else
@{tt}stategraph_runner@{utt}. So the machines you make in the panel
(@{tt}data/stategraph/machines@{utt}) run with the default runner and its
few tools, and a plugin that ships machines ships their runner next to
them, in its own @{tt}agents/*.yaml@{utt} -- nothing of it goes into
stategraph's files:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    tickets_machine_runner:
      type: stategraph_runner       # inherits the default runner
      enabled: true
      runs_machines_in:
        - src/plugins/tickets/machines
      inject_params:                # added to its tool activities' arguments
        "ticket_store_*": {api_key: "${TICKET_KEY}"}
      agent_config:
        tools:
          allowed:
            - "+ticket_store/*"     # + adds to the inherited list
@{body}

@{bullet}The folder decides, never the machine: a machine anyone may write
cannot pick the runner with the most tools. A whole run -- its
submachines included -- runs with its root machine's runner, and
validation checks every file of it against that runner. The run keeps
the runner it started with: a resume and a fork of its snapshot run with
it again, a fork onto the current definition gets the runner of where the
machine is now.
@{bullet}Folders are relative to the project, globs allowed; the deepest
named folder that holds a machine wins. Two runners naming the same
folder: no machine there can be started until one of them lets go --
validation says which two (SG007). A run started before keeps the runner
it started with.
@{bullet}The whole tree runs with its root's runner: a file of it that lies
in a writable folder another runner (or none) has -- an import by
machine id finds the writable folder first -- is SG007 for a machine with
a runner of its own. Import such machines from the machine's folder.
@{bullet}@{tt}runs_machines_in@{utt} is not inherited: a runner derived
from another claims no folder by accident.
@{bullet}A runner's @{tt}inject_params@{utt} come after the instance's and
win where both set a param; the instance's other params stay.
@{bullet}The panel shows a machine's runner in its overview, and the tool
fields offer that runner's tools; @{tt}stategraph_catalog@{utt} takes the
machine as @{tt}machine_id@{utt}.
@{bullet}A new runner needs a restart, like every new agent.

An agent that should use the tools gets them in its allowlist, one by one
or all: @{tt}"+stategraph/*"@{utt}.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default in code | Meaning
machine_dirs | stategraph/machines in the data folder, src/plugins*/*/machines | machine folders in search order; the first with an id wins
writable_machine_dirs | stategraph/machines in the data folder | folders whose machines may be edited, no globs; new ones go into the first
runs_db | stategraph/runs.db in the data folder | runs and their journals (SQLite)
runner_agent | stategraph_runner | the default runner: of every machine whose folder no runner claims
allowed_users | [] | fnmatch patterns of users who may validate, save, run and control besides admins
inject_params | {} | secrets for every tool activity, see above; one plugin's go into its runner
default_max_wait | 600 | seconds run_machine and get_run wait with wait finish
public_url | "" | the app's URL as the outside reaches it: the base of callback URLs
schedules | [] | see @{" Schedules " link schedules}
@{body}

Needs @{tt}ruamel.yaml@{utt}, @{tt}networkx@{utt} and
@{tt}jsonschema@{utt}. Each machine's layout is a
@{tt}<id>.layout.json@{utt} next to its file.
@endnode

@node security "Security"
@{h1}Security

Machines contain Python that runs in the API process with the rights of
plugin code, and they run agents and tools. So:

@{bullet}The panel and its API are for admins: the route rule
@{tt}/plugins/stategraph/*@{utt} in @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt}, and
the plugin checks the role itself as well.
@{bullet}With auth on, the tools that validate, save, run, control and send
events need an admin or a user in @{tt}allowed_users@{utt}. Validating
and saving never run a machine's Python module; only a run does.
@{bullet}Runs belong to their user (see @{" Runs " link runs}).
@{bullet}An @{tt}agent@{utt} activity runs only agents the file names --
literally, or as a param with an enum -- and none that reaches machines:
not a runner, not a machine agent, not one whose tools include a
stategraph tool beyond the read-only ones (catalog, list, get), nor one
that can start such an agent through its SAM. Checked when validating and again at run time; the same holds
for the tool of a @{tt}tool@{utt} activity.
@{bullet}@{tt}python:@{utt} and @{tt}imports:@{utt} resolve only inside the
machine folders.
@{bullet}A mock-only run has no connection to agents or tools at all.

@{h2}Opening the callback route

Callback URLs work only once an operator lets anonymous callers reach
their path. In @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt}, add one entry to each of
two lists -- to @{tt}auth.endpoint_security.rules@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
      - pattern: "/plugins/stategraph/callback*"
        policy: "allow_anonymous"
@{body}

and to @{tt}auth.plugin_security.endpoint_rules@{utt}, above the
@{tt}/plugins/stategraph/*@{utt} rule (the first rule that matches
wins):

@{code yaml}
      - pattern: "/plugins/stategraph/callback*"
        policy: "allow_anonymous"
@{body}

Never as a block in @{tt}config/local.yaml@{utt}: a list there replaces
the whole list of the files before it, and the login rules
(@{tt}/auth/*@{utt}) and every admin rule would be gone.

With @{tt}network.remote_paths@{utt} set, a caller on another machine
reaches only the paths listed there: add
@{tt}"/plugins/stategraph/callback"@{utt}.
@endnode
